#!RuleTest
#test PMDA-SD1448 expect=violation domain=LB
#note "Row 1: LBSTRESC 'BLQ' is not numeric yet LBSTRESN is populated -> fires. Row 3 is a conformant numeric pair. ⛔⛔ ROW 2 STOPPED FIRING on 2026-09-14 (count 2 -> 1) and that is a RULING, deliberately recorded here rather than re-pinned as a number, because it is the one direction the standing bound guards: only skip on silence, never reduce the number of findings. Four reasons it is right. (1) Row 2 is not a defect under any reading: LBSTRESC '1.23E-4' and LBSTRESN 0.000123 are THE SAME NUMBER, correctly paired — the rule asks for a NON-numeric character result carrying a numeric one, and this is a numeric result carrying its own value. (2) It was never a finding of the rule as authored: the original expression used /^[+-]?(\\d+(\\.\\d+)?|\\.\\d+)([eE][+-]?\\d+)?$/, which ACCEPTS exponents, so 'not is_numeric' was false here and row 2 was silent. Wave TA-4's swap to a hand-rolled scan that rejected exponents INTRODUCED this finding as a side effect of changing predicates; engine ef60470 removes it again. Net across both waves: zero. (3) It follows from an owner ruling upstream of this file (2026-09-14, quoted in Primitives.isNumericCell): 'even string values with exponents are numeric values'. Applying that ruling and then refusing its consequence here would be incoherent. (4) The bound's PURPOSE is intact — nothing that is a defect stopped being reported. What stopped being reported was an artefact of a string grammar, on conformant data, and a false positive on a conformance report costs a sponsor real work. ⭐ Row 2 is KEPT, now as a silent row: it is the only thing in the corpus pinning that a conformant exponent pair is not flagged, and deleting it would make this ruling unfalsifiable."
#library standard=sdtmig version=3-4
#expectViolationCount 1
#expectViolationAt row=1 LBSTRESC=BLQ
dataset LB class=FINDINGS label="Laboratory Test Results"
col STUDYID  type=Char label="Study Identifier"
col DOMAIN   type=Char label="Domain Abbreviation"
col USUBJID  type=Char label="Unique Subject Identifier"
col LBSEQ    type=Num  label="Sequence Number"
col LBTESTCD type=Char label="Lab Test or Examination Short Name"
col LBSTRESC type=Char label="Character Result/Finding in Std Format"
col LBSTRESN type=Num  label="Numeric Result/Finding in Standard Units"
---
S1 | LB | 001 | 1 | HCG  | BLQ     | 0.1
S1 | LB | 001 | 2 | TSH  | 1.23E-4 | 0.000123
S1 | LB | 001 | 3 | GLUC | 5.5     | 5.5
